  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...    \nRelatively quiet weather should continue into the beginning of \nnext week as quasi-zonal flow aloft continues. However, a building\nSLP gradient across the the Baja Spine/San Diego Mountains could \nbegin to induce strong westerly gap winds in the typically prone \nI-8 corridor near Mountain Springs as early as Sunday night/early \nMonday morning. Otherwise, generally light winds and afternoon \nhighs in the middle 70s across the lower deserts should prevail \nMonday.\n\nThere is building model consensus in a potent Pacific trough \ndeveloping off the West Coast early next week, which will then \nprogress through the Southwest US during the middle of the week. A\nlarge majority of ensemble members now show the trough digging \nfar enough south to pull decent moisture into the region - \noverall, a favorable trajectory to support rain if ascent \nmechanisms align. The evolution of this system is just now \nstarting to come into better focus. The latest global guidance is \nin decent agreement that rain chances will likely come in 2 \nseparate periods, one associated with an initial Pacific cold \nfront with WAA/saturated isentropic ascent in a narrow band out \nahead of it and a more concentrated area of frontogenetical \nforcing trailing behind, followed by a second period likely \nsometime on Wednesday associated with the passage of the center of\nthe upper low. These details are certainly subject to change.\n\nRainfall potential is still highly uncertain this far out, but \nWPC QPF has trended up once again in the latest update, now \nshowing average rain totals on the order of 0.75-1.00\" across the \nSouth- Central AZ lower deserts through the entire event. As is \ntypical, there is a strong gradient towards the higher terrain to \nthe north and east of Phoenix, a consequence of orographic \nenhancement. Experience suggests that rain shadowing effects are \nunderplayed in the WPC QPF for far Southwest AZ and Southeast CA, \nand so the totals out there may underperform compared to the \ncurrent forecast (average totals between 0.20-0.50\"). Some \nisolated/embedded thunderstorm activity cannot be ruled out during\nthis event, with the most likely timing coinciding with the first\nperiod, along/ahead of the front. Model soundings currently \nindicate weak instability (100-250 J/kg MUCAPE) at this time, but \nif cooler air aloft moves in quickly enough, lapse rates may \nbecome more favorable. The coldest air aloft will come with the \npassage of the upper low on Wednesday, but moisture profiles do \nnot look as robust, and the exact track of the low will determine \nwhere this \"second period\" of precipitation sets up. Aside from \nrain chances, widespread gusty winds will be a concern with this \nupcoming system. Height packing ahead of the trough and a \ncyclonically curved attendant jet streak will support deep, strong\nsouthwesterly flow. Mixing during the afternoon on Tuesday should\ntap this higher momentum air, resulting in gusts between 25-40 \nmph across the lower deserts, locally higher over ridgetops. Model\nconsensus currently shows the front passing through Tuesday \nnight, such that by Wednesday, gradients will begin to relax, and \nonly lingering breeziness should remain for some areas. \n\nBy Thursday, the Pacific low will have weakened substantially and \nprogressed east of the region, allowing an opportunity for quick \nclearing and a period with weak temperature/pressure gradients \nacross the region. Light winds and widespread wetting rainfall could \nset the stage for fog to develop across portions of the lower \ndeserts, but this will be highly dependent on cloud cover. \nRegardless, cooler temperatures behind the front will prevail \nstarting Wednesday, with latest NBM showing temperatures dropping \nclose to daily normals. A period of quieter weather should follow \nthe passage of this Pacific trough, though the active pattern may \nsoon return thereafter (potentially by the weekend).",
